      ],
      "variant_group": "field::ca28b5821c95cdc982fc0ab600c254970533854851acd6bb013461cd69f73c73",
      "multiple_values_in_field": false
    },
    "atom::0f20a9beca06842928086873": {
      "atom_id": "atom::0f20a9beca06842928086873",
      "source_handle": "P0416",
      "field_path": [
        "study_summary_A",
        "problem_or_question"
      ],
      "value": "The review addresses the gap between clinical observations of microbiome-ICI associations and the lack of robust, human-relevant preclinical models to mechanistically dissect these interactions. It questions how current models fail to capture the complexity of the tumor-immune-microbiome axis and proposes immune-reactive organoids as a solution to predict patient-specific responses and understand underlying mechanisms.",
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::7b870da08818d8b1ba61ac26d851f520a51023deedd0a47cf15dc46b53c57bac",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "problem_or_question"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::719fe436dd07bbf6a13490b1e11e07dfe00153e58e7cf867b67e429199757a96",
      "multiple_values_in_field": false
    },
    "atom::67a86efa0ce7593078af701a": {
      "atom_id": "atom::67a86efa0ce7593078af701a",
      "source_handle": "P0416",
      "field_path": [
        "study_summary_A",
        "research_scope"
      ],
      "value": "The review focuses on the intersection of cancer immunotherapy, specifically immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) in solid tumors, and the gut microbiome. It evaluates various preclinical models, with a primary emphasis on immune-reactive tumor organoids as a platform to study these interactions.",
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::7b870da08818d8b1ba61ac26d851f520a51023deedd0a47cf15dc46b53c57bac",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "research_scope"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::ffa3a96aae4bf9250e67b46a535e5394b08a7aa04083c40c4e432a0970405148",
      "multiple_values_in_field": false
    },
    "atom::f7fca2ab6a28ec9acd7cc403": {
      "atom_id": "atom::f7fca2ab6a28ec9acd7cc403",
      "source_handle": "P0416",
      "field_path": [
        "study_summary_A",
        "work_summary"
      ],
      "value": "This paper is a narrative review that argues for the utility of immune-reactive tumor organoids in deciphering how the microbiome influences cancer immunity and response to immunotherapies. It begins by outlining the clinical evidence linking gut microbiota composition to ICI outcomes in solid tumors, summarizing key findings from multiple clinical trials regarding specific bacterial taxa and metabolites. The review then critiques existing modeling systems (2D cultures and animal models), highlighting their limitations in capturing human-specific immune-microbiome-tumor dynamics. It proposes immune-reactive organoids—created either by retaining endogenous immune cells or co-culturing autologous immune cells with tumor organoids—as a superior ex vivo platform. The authors discuss recent experimental data using these organoid systems to test microbial metabolites and checkpoint blockade efficacy, assessing their validity for personalized medicine and mechanistic insight.",
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::7b870da08818d8b1ba61ac26d851f520a51023deedd0a47cf15dc46b53c57bac",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "work_summary"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::07916d829ecad13106cfbb1989e55f1defa5ceb267254e0547e27a2c35263280",
      "multiple_values_in_field": false
    },
    "atom::710160eba78c40d56ceac34b": {
      "atom_id": "atom::710160eba78c40d56ceac34b",
      "source_handle": "P0416",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "planning_summary"
      ],
      "value": "This review serves as a critical background source for understanding why advanced in vitro models like immune-reactive organoids are necessary to study microbiome-ICI interactions. It provides a curated summary of clinical associations between specific gut bacteria/metabolites and ICI efficacy in solid tumors, which helps contextualize the biological phenomena that these novel models aim to replicate. It also offers a comparative analysis of modeling platforms, justifying the shift towards organoid-based approaches for mechanistic studies.",
      "role": "planner_interpretation",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::7b870da08818d8b1ba61ac26d851f520a51023deedd0a47cf15dc46b53c57bac",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "planning_summary"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::241bebb40a2df867e25b109f6e675e79fa1d7be31e90f35ebd01d98ebf3835ca",
      "multiple_values_in_field": false
    },
    "atom::da420b78163a0f5db3eb844c": {
      "atom_id": "atom::da420b78163a0f5db3eb844c",
      "source_handle": "P0416",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "facet_contributions"
      ],
      "value": {
        "boundaries": "The review notes that biomarkers are cohort-dependent and no single species is universally predictive. It does not provide causal proof for all observed associations, only correlations and some mechanistic hypotheses.",
        "contribution": "Summarizes clinical evidence showing that specific gut microbiota profiles (e.g., Akkermansia, Bifidobacterium) and higher diversity are associated with better response rates to ICIs in solid tumors like melanoma and NSCLC. It details interventions like FMT that restore responsiveness.",
        "facet_id": "F1"
      },
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::7b870da08818d8b1ba61ac26d851f520a51023deedd0a47cf15dc46b53c57bac",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions",
            "0"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::19eccb04e03f13bb8a5402387b767567270a8a7956bf5e29eaa0a8828a044c0a",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::0fc98118e42e92412be4cfcb": {
      "atom_id": "atom::0fc98118e42e92412be4cfcb",
      "source_handle": "P0416",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "facet_contributions",
        "possible_uses"
      ],
      "value": "Use to establish the clinical baseline of microbiome-ICI correlation in solid tumors. Cite to support the existence of a microbiome effect on ICI efficacy before discussing mechanistic models.",
      "role": "planning",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::7b870da08818d8b1ba61ac26d851f520a51023deedd0a47cf15dc46b53c57bac",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions",
            "0",
            "possible_uses"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::574a1214b560486a261f11665fa22183c3ac300c862a62ff8133c9a4f3cce2bf",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::bb61b58fb0d1d6804d9ba48d": {
      "atom_id": "atom::bb61b58fb0d1d6804d9ba48d",
      "source_handle": "P0416",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "facet_contributions"
      ],
      "value": {
        "boundaries": "The mechanistic explanations are largely derived from cited studies or theoretical frameworks; the review itself does not perform new mechanistic experiments. The link between specific metabolites and specific immune cell subsets in organoids is presented as an emerging capability rather than a settled consensus.",
        "contribution": "Describes proposed mechanisms such as antigen mimicry, pattern recognition receptor activation, and metabolite-mediated immune modulation (e.g., SCFAs promoting T-cell differentiation). It highlights how immune-reactive organoids can be used to test these mechanisms ex vivo.",
        "facet_id": "F2"
      },
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::7b870da08818d8b1ba61ac26d851f520a51023deedd0a47cf15dc46b53c57bac",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions",
            "1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::19eccb04e03f13bb8a5402387b767567270a8a7956bf5e29eaa0a8828a044c0a",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::867a5e9199da650f65fac049": {
      "atom_id": "atom::867a5e9199da650f65fac049",
      "source_handle": "P0416",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "facet_contributions",
        "possible_uses"
      ],
      "value": "Use to explain the biological plausibility of microbiome effects on ICI. Use the organoid methodology section to illustrate how these mechanisms can be studied in a controlled, human-relevant setting, contrasting with animal model limitations.",
      "role": "planning",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::7b870da08818d8b1ba61ac26d851f520a51023deedd0a47cf15dc46b53c57bac",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions",
            "1",
            "possible_uses"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::574a1214b560486a261f11665fa22183c3ac300c862a62ff8133c9a4f3cce2bf",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
